  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 255 PM EDT Mon Oct 5 2026\n\n*** MILD THROUGH TOMORROW, PATCHY FROST(?) TONIGHT ***\n\nSeasonably mild conditions continue over central Kentucky and \nsouthern Indiana today as high pressure to our north maintains cool, \nnortherly breezes. At 245pm EDT, temperatures were in the low 70s, \nand dewpoints, brought down by deep mixing into a dry layer above \n900mb, were in the upper 30s to mid 40s. This dry airmass and lack \nof any clouds will favor efficient radiational losses overnight, \nthough some uncertainty exists in winds as high pressure settles \nover the Appalachians, leaving a slight gradient across our area. In \nsheltered valleys, particularly across the Bluegrass area where winds \nwill be lighter closer to the high pressure center, temperatures may \n(HREF probabilities are 15-30%) fall into the upper 30s tonight. \nHowever, any frost would be extremely isolated, as indicated by \nNBM's <3% probabilities for temperatures less than 37 degF.\n\n*** SLIGHTLY WARMER, REMAINING DRY INTO SATURDAY ***\n\nAs the surface high shifts east across the Mid-Atlantic, the \npressure gradient will weaken across the area tomorrow favoring \nlight and variable winds, while sunny skies allow temperatures to \nclimb into the mid to upper 70s (warmest south). Return flow out of \nthe southwest will develop tomorrow night into Wednesday, advecting \na slightly warmer airmass into the region. Given mixing up to around \n850mb, HREF mean 850mb temps around 15 degC suggests highs will \nclimb into the lower to mid 80s (warmest west), however this deep \nmixing will also bring dry air down to the surface (min RH values 25-\n35%) - keeping heat indices cooler than actual air temperatures.\n\nA decaying cool front will sink south into central/southern Indiana \non Thursday, with surface high pressure once again shifting from the \nMidwest to Appalachians in its wake. Meanwhile, the warm and dry \nairmass is likely to linger over our area into the weekend with raw \nand calibrated guidance maintaining highs in the upper 70s to low \n80s through Saturday (around 5-7 degrees above \"normal\").\n\n*** TROPICAL REMNANTS TO BRING BENEFICIAL RAIN(?) BY SUNDAY ***\n\nThere remains considerable uncertainty surrounding the evolution and \ntrack of a disturbance over the southwestern Gulf. NHC suggests \nthere is a 60% (80%) chance of development into a Tropical Cyclone \nover the next 2 (7) days, however member low locations from both the \nGEFS and EPS ensembles exhibit considerable spread in its eventual \ntrack with some members bringing it northward into our neck of the \nwoods and others shunting it across the Southeast US. If the low \ntracks near/through the local area, the deep Gulf Moisture \nassociated with this system suggests we'd see a hefty rain event - \nperhaps ending the developing drought. However, if it passes to the \neast or west, the weekend would likely remain dry afterall. At this \ntime, our forecast features a 40-50% chance of measurable rain, \nwhile NBM chances for more than 2 inches range from 10% north of I-\n64 to 25% over central/southern Kentucky.",
